WEBVTT

NOTE
Journey: Advocates — Week 22 Leader Video
Unit 6, Lesson 2: Archaeology

1
00:00:06.100 --> 00:00:11.220
So this week we're talking about archaeology and we asked your students in the introduction

2
00:00:11.220 --> 00:00:17.140
activity to produce their signature, kind of a strange thing to do, right?

3
00:00:17.140 --> 00:00:19.460
So do your signature.

4
00:00:19.460 --> 00:00:24.100
There's my mess of a signature and we asked them to do that multiple times.

5
00:00:24.100 --> 00:00:26.860
Well, why?

6
00:00:26.860 --> 00:00:33.720
Because when an archaeologist is digging and what archaeologists do is if you have

7
00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:39.080
an excavation site, you have to dig below.

8
00:00:39.080 --> 00:00:43.280
Well, what happens when a city falls?

9
00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:46.240
It crumbles to the ground, it all falls in.

10
00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:52.880
Well, it's not like today where if this whole entire building came down, then we would clear

11
00:00:52.880 --> 00:00:59.840
the rubble, send it to recycling facilities and rebuild and level the ground and start

12
00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:00.840
all over again.

13
00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:03.080
They didn't have those capabilities.

14
00:01:03.080 --> 00:01:10.000
So if a city fell and all the rubble sat here, they would simply level off the rubble and

15
00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:12.000
build a new city right on top.

16
00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:17.600
And we keep doing that year after year after year or decade after decade or whatever the

17
00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:19.000
case may be.

18
00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:24.080
So as archaeologists are digging, they're digging below each of these levels, each is

19
00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:29.120
called, it's an archaeological tell, is what they call it.

20
00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:33.840
And they're digging into, well, they have to use clues to figure out if they started

21
00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:39.000
digging here and they found something, that's going to be from a different time period

22
00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:43.720
than if they dug down here and found something.

23
00:01:43.720 --> 00:01:50.800
So the signature is a way for your students to help understand that if they found a

24
00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:56.920
piece of paper with my signature on it and they figured out that it was me with this big

25
00:01:56.920 --> 00:02:02.800
mess of a signature, they would know that, let's see, based on the fact that I used an

26
00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:07.760
expo marker, the fact that I'm on a white board, the fact that it's black, all these

27
00:02:07.760 --> 00:02:14.400
factors, plus they would connect it to me, could figure out roughly the date or at

28
00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:17.680
least close to when this actually happened.

29
00:02:17.680 --> 00:02:20.440
Let me give you another example.

30
00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:25.640
This is the Hulkbuster and full disclosure, this is mine, not my kids.

31
00:02:25.640 --> 00:02:29.380
If this whole building collapsed, let's say we were in the ancient times and they had

32
00:02:29.380 --> 00:02:35.760
Legos in ancient times, and this whole building collapsed and they found pieces of this and

33
00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:39.800
they put it back together and discovered that it was this.

34
00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:45.360
Based on the kind of Lego this is, based on the character, the fact that this is

35
00:02:45.400 --> 00:02:51.480
Iron Man and the Hulkbuster suit, that they would pretty much know exactly when this

36
00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:59.680
Lego came out, roughly when it got built and what era they're currently digging in.

37
00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:02.240
So that's the importance and kind of what archaeologists do.

38
00:03:02.240 --> 00:03:05.720
And we want your students to start thinking about what that looks like, okay, this isn't

39
00:03:05.720 --> 00:03:10.200
necessarily Indiana Jones kind of stuff, but it gives us a good idea of how this

40
00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:11.200
is done.

41
00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:18.840
So when they come across biblical artifacts, what does that mean for the biblical text?

42
00:03:18.840 --> 00:03:24.360
So for your storyteller, this is a really cool opportunity to have someone come in.

43
00:03:24.360 --> 00:03:27.560
You don't have to have an archaeologist, you don't have to have anybody that has

44
00:03:27.560 --> 00:03:30.120
an amazing discovery.

45
00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:32.520
If you can, that would be awesome.

46
00:03:32.520 --> 00:03:36.680
If you happen to have somebody in a local seminary that has done some traveling to

47
00:03:36.680 --> 00:03:39.200
the Middle East and Jerusalem and whatnot, that'd be awesome.

48
00:03:39.200 --> 00:03:42.640
But if not, that's okay, don't worry, like you have to have that.

49
00:03:42.640 --> 00:03:48.280
You could simply have somebody that was rummaging around in grandma's attic and found

50
00:03:48.280 --> 00:03:51.520
a really important family heirloom.

51
00:03:51.520 --> 00:03:58.120
Years ago, my grandma was moving from her condo to a long-term care facility because

52
00:03:58.120 --> 00:04:03.720
she was getting ill and needed to be watched, pretty much 24-7.

53
00:04:03.720 --> 00:04:06.920
So my family all got together and we were kind of going through all of our stuff.

54
00:04:06.920 --> 00:04:11.600
That's a little bit weird when she's sitting there watching us go through all of her stuff.

55
00:04:11.600 --> 00:04:15.840
But it was fascinating because we found all these really cool artifacts and there were

56
00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:20.520
stories behind it and it was stuff that reminded my dad of his childhood and kind of what that

57
00:04:20.520 --> 00:04:25.480
meant and we talked about really interesting stories and we had a really great time walking

58
00:04:25.480 --> 00:04:28.600
down those memories.

59
00:04:28.600 --> 00:04:34.640
But if you could just find a storyteller to even tell that story, it helps connect

60
00:04:34.680 --> 00:04:40.640
the importance of discovery and archaeology and artifacts and all those pieces of history

61
00:04:40.640 --> 00:04:44.240
with what we can know about the Bible.

62
00:04:44.240 --> 00:04:50.280
Because my dad can tell me a story, but when he connects it with an artifact or something

63
00:04:50.280 --> 00:04:55.080
from his past, that story becomes that much more reliable, because it could just be

64
00:04:55.080 --> 00:05:00.720
he remembered it wrong, but it also becomes that much more powerful because I'm understanding

65
00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:04.560
that what he's telling me is now actually history.

66
00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:09.120
And that's exactly what we're hoping your students get when they look at archaeology

67
00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:10.360
and scripture.

68
00:05:10.360 --> 00:05:13.440
So for small groups, your focus is simple.

69
00:05:13.440 --> 00:05:21.440
I want you to focus on helping students see that this Bible is also history.

70
00:05:21.440 --> 00:05:23.560
Yeah, it's the word of God.

71
00:05:23.560 --> 00:05:25.000
Yes, it's inspired.

72
00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:30.760
Yes, it's completely accurate in every way because God made it that way.

73
00:05:30.800 --> 00:05:36.840
But God also made this a accurate reflection of history.

74
00:05:36.840 --> 00:05:40.000
What goes on in here is actually historical.

75
00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:45.480
We have found coins that give us indication that David actually was a king.

76
00:05:45.480 --> 00:05:50.840
And we've seen things from the New Testament that have the inscription of Pontius Pilate

77
00:05:50.840 --> 00:05:54.280
on it that remind us that Pontius Pilate was an actual person.

78
00:05:54.280 --> 00:05:56.200
He was an actual governor.

79
00:05:56.200 --> 00:06:02.840
He actually was the one responsible during Jesus's crucifixion.

80
00:06:02.840 --> 00:06:04.920
There is history behind it all.

81
00:06:04.920 --> 00:06:09.440
And we want your students to kind of drill that in, but I also want you to pay attention

82
00:06:09.440 --> 00:06:12.880
to their questions because now that they're starting to see scripture as something that's

83
00:06:12.880 --> 00:06:16.880
a little bit more important than maybe they thought it was, there's going to be some

84
00:06:16.880 --> 00:06:20.920
pushback and some thought that, well, isn't this just a bunch of stories and myths

85
00:06:20.920 --> 00:06:23.160
that maybe people thought were true?

86
00:06:23.160 --> 00:06:27.800
And there's some sort of evidence that maybe they just kind of messed around with history

87
00:06:27.800 --> 00:06:31.120
and they just kind of messed around with the truth a little bit.

88
00:06:31.120 --> 00:06:35.280
There's going to be questions and you need to be ready to answer those questions and

89
00:06:35.280 --> 00:06:37.680
talk through them a little bit.

90
00:06:37.680 --> 00:06:41.960
But I want you to continue to walk students down that road of helping them understand

91
00:06:41.960 --> 00:06:46.880
and see the importance of this book and what it means for their lives.

92
00:06:46.880 --> 00:06:50.320
So I'm going to leave you with one final question that I want you to leave your

93
00:06:50.320 --> 00:06:53.080
students with.

94
00:06:53.080 --> 00:07:01.120
Two weeks in, what does this information from the biblical reliability to archaeological

95
00:07:01.120 --> 00:07:07.480
evidence do for how you see the Bible?

96
00:07:07.480 --> 00:07:11.480
Think about that for yourself because if you didn't know some of this information

97
00:07:11.480 --> 00:07:16.320
prior to these last couple of weeks and now moving forward with your students,

98
00:07:16.320 --> 00:07:18.440
think about that with yourself.

99
00:07:18.440 --> 00:07:22.520
Does this change how you see it and don't be afraid to share this with your students

100
00:07:22.560 --> 00:07:25.200
and help them see like, hey, I didn't know either.

101
00:07:25.200 --> 00:07:26.720
I didn't maybe I didn't know all of this.

102
00:07:26.720 --> 00:07:27.880
Maybe I'm not.

103
00:07:27.880 --> 00:07:31.000
This is new information for you as well.

104
00:07:31.000 --> 00:07:32.640
Share that with your students.

105
00:07:32.640 --> 00:07:37.320
Walk alongside them, especially encourage your small group leaders to do the same

106
00:07:37.320 --> 00:07:42.160
thing so that they can see that this isn't something that they ought to know

107
00:07:42.160 --> 00:07:45.000
and everybody should know and they're just kind of out of the loop that

108
00:07:45.000 --> 00:07:47.200
this is a journey for all of you.

109
00:07:47.200 --> 00:07:51.680
And the more you understand, the greater that this book becomes.

